module test.fixture.native_projection_projected

// RETARGETED 2026-11-01 into `test.fixture`, a namespace the declared layout has a row for: an entry must
// lie in a declared namespace (v2.compiler.module_acquisition acquisition_entry_requests), and rows are
// emitted from the governed trees, so a `fixture.*` namespace can never be a row
// (dag/gunbc/source_layout_namespaces.dag). No repository module of this name exists, in this or any tree.

// THE NATIVE EMIT VERB'S WALKED SUBJECT FOR "A PROJECTION IS NOT A MODULE REFERENCE"
// (src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run/native_lane_runner.rs walk_native_acquisition_projection_controls).
//
// `product.y` below is a field projection: `product` is the parameter this function binds, `y` its field. It
// is spelled exactly as the module `product.y` would be, and the tree this file is read from does store a
// module of that name (product/y.dag beside this root's other files), so the control can tell a compiler
// that decides what `product.y` means from the lexical frames at the site from one that decides it from which
// files exist. The walk reads this entry from three trees that differ only in that module (absent,
// malformed, well-formed) and requires the closure the door reports to be this module alone, with the same
// status and the same carrier, in all three.
//
// THE OBSERVATION IS THE READ SET, NOT THIS ENTRY'S EMISSION. What the native route's inference makes of the
// body is another fact, owned by the inference witnesses, so the walk pins no verdict for it beyond its being
// the same in all three trees. The head is a parameter because the root-bound arm of the same fact is held by
// v2.test.claim.lexical_scope a_module_level_value_is_bound_at_the_root, and the walk holds the frame arm
// through the built door.
type Holder {
  y: Int
}

fn native_projection_read(product: Holder) -> Int {
  product.y
}
